// TypeResolution — the Maxon-tier `resolveTypes` pass. Replaces every
// unresolved / named `MaxonType` with a concrete one so that `lowerMaxonToStd`
// (whose `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on `named`/`unresolved`) never sees an
// unresolved type, and keeps the `funcReturnTypes` registry in sync with the
// resolved module so SemanticCheck reads resolved types.
//
// ⭐ **THE CASCADE BELOW IS `denotedNamedType`, AND IT HAS THREE READERS, NOT ONE.** "What does this
// name denote?" is asked by this pass (whose `unknown` verdict IS its E3011) AND by the two places a
// program writes an `as`-cast target — `Parser.applyCast` for a body cast and `Parser.applyConstCast`
// for a top-level `let`'s. Those two used to ask nothing at all: `parseTypeReference` hands back a
// bare `MaxonType.named` for every type it cannot answer syntactically, `maxonTypeTag` reads all of
// them as one tag, and so `5 as CompletelyMadeUpNameXyz` compiled and returned 5. The cure is the ONE
// list, asked from all three — never a second cascade in the parser, which is this project's
// signature bug (one fact written down twice) aimed at the question where the two copies would look
// most alike.
//
// It reads the WHOLE-PROGRAM signature index rather than the `Project` registries, and that is what
// makes it askable from the parser at all (the parser holds no `Project` — the pure-parser
// invariant). The two hold the same declarations, folded from the same artifacts through the same
// doors: `upsertDeclaredEnum` is the single write of BOTH enum registries and `createEnumRegistry`
// seeds both, and the parser has ALREADY been deciding struct-ness from the index for every `named`
// parameter and return type (`Parser.resolveNamedStruct`), so reading it here makes resolution agree
// with the parser rather than merely with itself.
//
// A named type resolves through FOUR sources, in order:
//
//   1. The BUILTIN alias `ExitCode`, recognized by NAME — WITHOUT loading the stdlib
//      (full stdlib type resolution is deferred until programs call stdlib functions).
//      It resolves to `MaxonType.exitCode`, a width-free tag whose unsigned-32-bit
//      width is assigned at the Maxon→Std boundary (see `maxonTypeToStdType`).
//   2. The project's DECLARED STRUCT registry — every `type Point` the parser declared. It
//      resolves to `MaxonType.structRef(id)`, KEEPING the name id, which is the opposite of
//      what an alias does and is the whole distinction between the two: an alias creates no
//      nominal identity, and a `type` is nothing BUT one. `Point` and `Rect` are different
//      types however identical their fields, and the id is what says so.
//   3. The project's DECLARED ENUM/UNION registry — every `enum`/`union` the parser declared. It
//      resolves to `MaxonType.integer` (an enum value is its i64 tag), DROPPING the id like an
//      alias does: nothing downstream needs to know a value was a `Color`, because only `match`
//      inspects an enum and it reads the enum by name from the signature index at parse time.
//   4. The project's RANGED TYPEALIAS registry — every `typealias Score = int(0 to 100)`
//      the parser declared. An alias resolves to its UNDERLYING primitive and nothing
//      more: two aliases over the same range are the same type, because an alias
//      creates no nominal distinction. The range itself is not consulted here — a
//      narrowing conversion into a ranged alias is checked at run time, which is a
//      later milestone.
//
// A named type in none of the four is a USER error (a typo, or a type this slice does not
// have), not a compiler-invariant violation, and it is E3011. WHERE it is anchored is the
// asking reader's to decide, which is why `denotedNamedType` raises `UnknownTypeName` rather
// than reporting: a DECLARATION site reports unpositioned (nothing records a source span for a
// type reference in a signature or a field — see `resolveNamedType`), while a CAST site is
// anchored on the `as` the user wrote, because the parser holds that token when it asks.
//
// The integer literal `42` is already typed `integer` (→ i64) by the parser, so it
// needs no resolution.

// The one builtin alias resolved without the stdlib. Named constant, not a bare
// string — the alias spelling is a contract shared with the parser's interner. Grows
// into a builtin-alias table (Character, etc.) when the stdlib cone lands.
//
// EXPORTED because the parser's INTERFACE-RETURN resolver must claim it too: an interface stores its
// method return type as a rendered source STRING, so `Parser.interfaceReturnMaxonType` re-derives the
// MaxonType from the name and has to reach the same answer this cascade does. A second spelling of the
// bytes is what would let the two drift (P1.7a slice 2b-iv-B).
export let ExitCodeBuiltinName = b"ExitCode"

// `denotedNamedType`'s "this name is in none of the sources" answer. Payload-free — the caller
// already holds the name it asked about — so it is a scalar and the throw allocates nothing on a
// path taken once per named type reference in the program.
export enum UnknownTypeName implements Error
	notDeclared
end 'UnknownTypeName'

// Resolve every unresolved/named type in `module`'s function signatures to a
// concrete `MaxonType`, and re-sync each function's resolved return type into
// the project's `funcReturnTypes` registry (SemanticCheck's source of truth).
export function resolveTypes(project Project, module MaxonModule)
	// Fetched ONCE for the whole pass, not per named type: `denotedNamedType` reads it for every
	// reference, and the memoized query is a map probe that has no business being on that path. (It
	// used to be fetched inside `resolveNamedType`, on the rare qualified-alias arm only — this is
	// strictly fewer fetches, not more.)
	let signatures = try queryProgramSignatures(project) otherwise panic("resolveTypes: queryProgramSignatures failed — it is computed and memoized by compileToCodeResult before the pipeline runs, so it cannot fail fresh here")

	// Index-based so each function is a mutable binding (a `for x in ...` loop
	// var is immutable; field assignment through it is rejected).
	for i in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		var func = try module.functions.get(i) otherwise panic("resolveTypes: functions.get OOB at {i}")
		func.maxonReturnType = resolveReturnType(project, signatures: signatures, ret: func.maxonReturnType)

		var resolvedParams = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for pt in func.maxonParamTypes 'eachParam'
			resolvedParams.push(resolveType(project, signatures: signatures, t: pt))
		end 'eachParam'
		func.maxonParamTypes = resolvedParams

		project.funcReturnTypes.upsert(func.name, value: func.maxonReturnType)
		syncResolvedSignature(project, func: func)
	end 'eachFunc'

	checkStructFieldTypes(project, signatures: signatures)
	resolveFunctionAliasShapes(project, signatures: signatures)

	// M1 op value-types are all concrete (`integer` literals) — nothing to stamp.
	// Op-type resolution (typed constants whose type is a named alias) grows at M9.
end 'resolveTypes'

// ⭐ **THE FOURTH DECLARATION SITE — a FUNCTION TYPEALIAS's parameter and return types.** A
// `typealias Scaler = function(v Real) returns Real` names two types the three sweeps above never reach:
// the alias is not a function (so it has no `funcSignatures` entry to re-sync) and its types are stored
// interner-free (so nothing rewrote them at merge). This resolves them into `project.functionAliasShapes`,
// which is the form BOTH consumers need — the indirect call's argument widening and the function-type door
// agreement both ask about the REPRESENTATION, and a `named` alias answers neither until it is erased.
//
// ⚠ **IT RE-INTERNS THE STORED NAMES INTO `project.typeNames`** — the merged interner every id downstream of
// this pass lives in — because the alias deliberately carries names rather than ids (see FunctionTypeAlias).
// Interning is idempotent and the names came from files whose own interners already folded in, so this mints
// no new id in practice; it is what makes the alias's types comparable with a signature's at all.
//
// ⚠ Unlike `checkStructFieldTypes` it VALIDATES NOTHING. An undeclared name in a function alias's signature
// is already E3011 from the site that USES the alias (a parameter/field/return declared with it resolves
// through `resolveNamedType` on the way in); reporting it a second time here would double every such
// diagnostic. `resolveType`'s recovery answer for an unknown name is what lands in the shape, and the
// pipeline's error gate stops the build before anything reads it.
function resolveFunctionAliasShapes(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	for (name, alias) in project.functionTypeAliases 'eachAlias'
		var resolvedParams = MaxonTypeArray.create()
		for param in alias.paramTypes 'eachParam'
			resolvedParams.push(resolveType(project, signatures: signatures, t: functionAliasParamType(project, param: param)))
		end 'eachParam'
		project.functionAliasShapes.upsert(name, value: FunctionShape.create(resolvedParams, returnType: resolveReturnType(project, signatures: signatures, ret: functionAliasReturnType(project, alias: alias))))
	end 'eachAlias'
end 'resolveFunctionAliasShapes'

// Rebuild one stored parameter's `MaxonType` from its interner-free `(tag, name)` pair, re-interning the
// name into the project's merged interner. The twin of `functionAliasReturnType` below and of the parser's
// own `functionAliasReturnType`, which does the identical rebuild against the FILE's interner — the storage
// convention is one, so the rebuild is stated once per interner and nowhere else.
function functionAliasParamType(project Project, param FunctionAliasParam) returns MaxonType
	return maxonTypeOfTag(param.tag, nameId: functionAliasNameId(project, typeName: param.typeName))
end 'functionAliasParamType'

// Rebuild a function alias's declared RETURN type from its interner-free storage. `void` means the function
// type returns nothing.
function functionAliasReturnType(project Project, alias FunctionTypeAlias) returns MaxonReturnType
	if alias.returnTag == ValueTypeTag.void 'voidReturn'
		return MaxonReturnType.void
	end 'voidReturn'
	return MaxonReturnType.value(maxonTypeOfTag(alias.returnTag, nameId: functionAliasNameId(project, typeName: alias.returnTypeName)))
end 'functionAliasReturnType'

// The project-interner id behind one stored type NAME — `UnnamedTypeId` for a primitive, which carries none
// (the convention `FunctionTypeAlias.returnTypeName` and `FunctionAliasParam.typeName` share).
function functionAliasNameId(project Project, typeName ByteArray) returns TypeNameId
	return UnnamedTypeId if typeName.isEmpty() else project.typeNames.intern(typeName)
end 'functionAliasNameId'

// ⭐ **THE RESOLVED SHAPE OF THE FUNCTION TYPE A GOVERNING NAME DENOTES — THE ONE LOOKUP.** A function value
// carries its type as `MaxonType.function(id)`, whose id names either the FUNCTION it refers to (a
// `functionRef`, or a lifted closure) or the function TYPEALIAS a declared place was written with — and
// "the declared type is authoritative" means those two must reach one answer through one door. A declared
// function's resolved shape already exists as `funcSignatures` + `funcReturnTypes` (`resolveTypes` re-synced
// both), so it is assembled on demand rather than duplicated into a third registry; an alias's was
// materialized by `resolveFunctionAliasShapes` because nothing else holds it.
//
// The two registries are DISJOINT by construction — `Parser.requireUnreservedName`/`checkTypeSymbolNamespace`
// keep a type name and a function name from colliding — so the order they are tried in cannot matter.
//
// `undeclared` is the honest answer for a name in neither, and every caller must handle it rather than assume.
export union DeclaredFunctionShape
	declared(shape FunctionShape)
	undeclared
end 'DeclaredFunctionShape'

// The registries this reads travel as one `FunctionTypeRegistry` — see there for why all four move together.
export function declaredFunctionShapeOf(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, name ByteArray) returns DeclaredFunctionShape
	if let sig = try registry.funcSignatures.get(name) 'declaredFn'
		let returnType = try registry.funcReturnTypes.get(name) otherwise panic("declaredFunctionShapeOf: '{String.from(name)}' is in funcSignatures but not funcReturnTypes — the two are committed together per function and re-synced together by resolveTypes")
		return DeclaredFunctionShape.declared(FunctionShape.create(sig.paramTypes, returnType: returnType))
	end 'declaredFn'

	if let aliasShape = try registry.aliasShapes.get(name) 'alias'
		return DeclaredFunctionShape.declared(aliasShape)
	end 'alias'

	return DeclaredFunctionShape.undeclared
end 'declaredFunctionShapeOf'

// The registry a `Project` denotes, assembled where a pass first needs one. It copies four references and
// nothing else, so it is not a cache and cannot go stale against the project it was taken from within a pass.
export function functionTypeRegistryOf(project Project) returns FunctionTypeRegistry
	return FunctionTypeRegistry.create(project.funcSignatures, funcReturnTypes: project.funcReturnTypes, aliasShapes: project.functionAliasShapes, typeNames: project.typeNames)
end 'functionTypeRegistryOf'

// ⭐ **DO TWO FUNCTION TYPES AGREE? THE ONE ANSWER, for every door a function value passes through.** Same
// arity, and position for position the same RESOLVED type — which is exactly what a call through the value
// depends on, because a call site derives its argument register files, its argument count and its result
// register from the type it was DECLARED with and gets whatever the callee actually is.
//
// It is deliberately NOT `typesAgree`, and the difference is the whole rule: `typesAgree` puts `int` and
// `float` in one class (`5 + 2.0` must compile), so it would accept `fn(Real) returns Integer` standing in
// for `fn(Real) returns Real` — the very silent wrong answer this exists to close. Nor is it the NOMINAL
// display comparison `checkValueMergeSignatures` makes; see FunctionShape for why a door and a merge ask
// different questions and get different rules.
export function functionShapesAgree(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, declared FunctionShape, got FunctionShape) returns bool
	return shapesAgreeUnder(registry, declared: declared, got: got, pending: sharedEmptyFunctionTypePairs)
end 'functionShapesAgree'

// The rule proper, carrying the pairs of function-type names already under comparison further down the
// same descent (see `FunctionTypePair`). Split from the entry point above only so the cycle guard is one
// argument the three call sites never have to know about.
function shapesAgreeUnder(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, declared FunctionShape, got FunctionShape, pending FunctionTypePairArray) returns bool
	if declared.paramTypes.count() != got.paramTypes.count() 'arity'
		return false
	end 'arity'

	for i in 0 upto declared.paramTypes.count() 'eachParam'
		let declaredParam = try declared.paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("shapesAgreeUnder: declared.paramTypes.get OOB at {i} — the count was just read from the same array")
		let gotParam = try got.paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("shapesAgreeUnder: got.paramTypes.get OOB at {i} — the arity check above proved the two counts equal")
		if not resolvedTypesMatch(registry, declared: declaredParam, got: gotParam, pending: pending) 'paramMismatch'
			return false
		end 'paramMismatch'
	end 'eachParam'

	return returnSlotsMatch(registry, declared: declared.returnType, got: got.returnType, pending: pending)
end 'shapesAgreeUnder'

// The pending list a top-level comparison starts from. ONE object for the whole compile and NEVER mutated
// (`pendingWithPair` builds a fresh array), the same pay-for-use discipline `sharedEmptyBorrowFacts` carries:
// a nested function type is rare, so the overwhelming majority of comparisons must allocate nothing at all.
let sharedEmptyFunctionTypePairs = FunctionTypePairArray.create()

// Two resolved RETURN slots are the same slot: both return nothing, or both return the same type. A `void`
// meeting a value (or the reverse) is a real disagreement — a call site that expects a result register from
// a function that writes none reads whatever was there.
function returnSlotsMatch(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, declared MaxonReturnType, got MaxonReturnType, pending FunctionTypePairArray) returns bool
	match declared 'declaredSlot'
		void then return isVoidReturnType(got)
		value(declaredValue) then return not isVoidReturnType(got) and resolvedTypesMatch(registry, declared: declaredValue, got: returnedValueType(got), pending: pending)
	end 'declaredSlot'
end 'returnSlotsMatch'

// Is this return slot the void arm?
function isVoidReturnType(ret MaxonReturnType) returns bool
	return match ret 'r'
		void gives true
		value gives false
	end 'r'
end 'isVoidReturnType'

// The type a non-void return slot carries. `void` is a caller bug — every caller asks `isVoidReturnType`
// first — so it panics rather than inventing a type nothing returns.
function returnedValueType(ret MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonType
	match ret 'r'
		value(inner) then return inner
		void then panic("returnedValueType: a `void` return slot carries no type — ask isVoidReturnType first")
	end 'r'
end 'returnedValueType'

// Two POST-RESOLUTION types are the same type: the same tag, and — for the tags that still carry an
// identity after resolution — the same id. Both sides' ids live in `project.typeNames` (a signature's were
// remapped project-global at merge, an alias shape's were interned there by `resolveFunctionAliasShapes`),
// so the ids are directly comparable.
//
// Which tags carry an identity is stated as an exhaustive match rather than a list of "interesting" ones,
// so a tag added to `ValueTypeTag` cannot default into "compare the tag only" and silently let two distinct
// types agree.
function resolvedTypesMatch(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, declared MaxonType, got MaxonType, pending FunctionTypePairArray) returns bool
	let declaredTag = maxonTypeTag(declared)
	if declaredTag != maxonTypeTag(got) 'differentTag'
		return false
	end 'differentTag'

	// ⭐ **A NESTED FUNCTION TYPE IS COMPARED BY SHAPE, exactly as the outer one is.** It is the same rule
	// one level down, and it has to be: `typealias Outer = function(f InnerA) returns Integer` accepting
	// `function runner(f InnerB)` is legal (both reference compilers accept it) when `InnerA` and `InnerB`
	// resolve alike, and REFUSING it is not merely strict — the diagnostic renders both sides as the same
	// string, so it reads as a type failing to match itself. The converse matters just as much: if the two
	// nested types genuinely differ, `runner`'s own body calls `f` through `InnerB`'s signature while the
	// caller supplied an `InnerA`, and the wrong answer lands one level in where nothing else looks.
	if declaredTag == ValueTypeTag.function 'nestedFunctionType'
		return nestedFunctionTypesAgree(registry, declaredId: typeNameIdOf(declared), gotId: typeNameIdOf(got), pending: pending)
	end 'nestedFunctionType'

	let identifiedByName = match declaredTag 'k'
		// Every one of these IS its own type: there is nothing else about an `int`, a `float`, a `bool`, an
		// `ExitCode`, a `void` or a `String` to disagree about.
		boolean to float gives false
		exitCode to void gives false
		string gives false
		// A `named` type and a struct are NOMINAL — two of them share a tag and are still different types.
		// (`named` should not survive resolution, which erases a ranged alias and a boxed union to
		// `integer`; comparing its id costs nothing and is the correct answer if one does.)
		named gives true
		structRef gives true
		// Handled above, by descent — a `function`-tagged type never reaches this table.
		function gives true
		// A type PARAMETER's id is its POSITION and a generic INSTANCE's is its whole-program
		// `GenericInstanceId`; two function types agree only if they take the same instances in the same
		// opaque positions, so both are compared the same way for their own reasons.
		typeParameter to genericInstance gives true
	end 'k'

	return not identifiedByName or typeNameIdOf(declared) == typeNameIdOf(got)
end 'resolvedTypesMatch'

// Do the two function types these ids govern agree? The nested half of `functionShapesAgree`, and the same
// question, so it resolves both ids to shapes and asks that.
//
// The SAME id short-circuits to agreement before anything is looked up, which is what keeps this free on
// every ordinary program: a nested function parameter almost always spells the same alias on both sides, and
// that case allocates nothing and touches no registry. It is also what makes the cycle guard cheap — a
// SELF-referential alias meets itself here and never descends at all.
function nestedFunctionTypesAgree(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, declaredId TypeNameId, gotId TypeNameId, pending FunctionTypePairArray) returns bool
	if declaredId == gotId 'sameFunctionType'
		return true
	end 'sameFunctionType'

	let declaredName = functionTypeNameOf(registry, nameId: declaredId)
	let gotName = functionTypeNameOf(registry, nameId: gotId)
	if pairIsUnderComparison(pending, declaredName: declaredName, gotName: gotName) 'cycle'
		return true
	end 'cycle'

	return shapesAgreeUnder(registry, declared: requireFunctionShapeOf(registry, name: declaredName, side: "nested declared"), got: requireFunctionShapeOf(registry, name: gotName, side: "nested arriving"), pending: pendingWithPair(pending, declaredName: declaredName, gotName: gotName))
end 'nestedFunctionTypesAgree'

// ⭐ **THE NAME BEHIND A `function`-TAGGED TYPE'S ID — the ONE reader.** Every consumer of a function type
// holds an id (a value's `valueNameIds` entry, a declared `MaxonType.function`, a nested parameter) and needs
// the NAME, because that is what the registries are keyed by. All of them read it here: the ids are remapped
// project-global at merge, so one table answers for all three passes and none of them can resolve it its own
// way. SemanticCheck and LowerMaxonToStd each had a private copy of exactly this before.
export function functionTypeNameOf(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, nameId TypeNameId) returns ByteArray
	return try registry.typeNames.nameOf(nameId) otherwise panic("functionTypeNameOf: type-name id {nameId} is not in the project interner — a function type's name id is remapped project-global at merge (mergeArtifact)")
end 'functionTypeNameOf'

// ⭐ **THE SHAPE A GOVERNING NAME MUST HAVE — the ONE "it exists" wrapper**, for the four sites that require
// one: an indirect call's callee (asked once by SemanticCheck to check the arguments and once by the lowering
// to widen them), each end of a function-type door, and a function type nested inside another. `side` names
// which in the panic, so an invariant break says whether it was the callee, the place, the value or a nested
// parameter whose type went missing.
//
// It PANICS rather than reporting, and that is a statement about the parser rather than about the program:
// a `function` TAG is minted only from a name one of the three producers declares
// (`Parser.parseTypeReference` for a function alias, `emitFunctionRef` for a function,
// `parseClosureExpression` for a lifted closure), and every caller has already established that both its tags
// are `function`. A missing shape therefore means a producer was added without a registry entry, which is a
// compiler bug and never user input.
export function requireFunctionShapeOf(registry FunctionTypeRegistry, name ByteArray, side String) returns FunctionShape
	match declaredFunctionShapeOf(registry, name: name) 'shape'
		declared(shape) then return shape
		undeclared then panic("requireFunctionShapeOf: the {side} function type's governing name '{String.from(name)}' names neither a declared function/closure nor a function typealias — a `function` tag is minted only from one of the three (Parser.parseTypeReference / emitFunctionRef / parseClosureExpression)")
	end 'shape'
end 'requireFunctionShapeOf'

// Is this pair of names already being compared further up the same descent (see `FunctionTypePair`)? A linear
// scan, because the list holds one entry per level of function-type NESTING actually descended into — which
// is at most one on every program in the corpus and is bounded by the program's distinct function types.
function pairIsUnderComparison(pending FunctionTypePairArray, declaredName ByteArray, gotName ByteArray) returns bool
	for pair in pending 'eachPending'
		if pair.declaredName == declaredName and pair.gotName == gotName 'match'
			return true
		end 'match'
	end 'eachPending'
	return false
end 'pairIsUnderComparison'

// `pending` plus this pair, as a FRESH array — `sharedEmptyFunctionTypePairs` and every list above this one
// in the descent must stay untouched, because a sibling parameter compared after this one is not under it.
function pendingWithPair(pending FunctionTypePairArray, declaredName ByteArray, gotName ByteArray) returns FunctionTypePairArray
	var extended = FunctionTypePairArray.create()
	for pair in pending 'eachPending'
		extended.push(pair)
	end 'eachPending'
	extended.push(FunctionTypePair.create(declaredName, gotName: gotName))
	return extended
end 'pendingWithPair'

// ⭐ **THE THIRD DECLARATION SITE.** A program names a type in exactly three places — a PARAMETER,
// a RETURN type, and a FIELD — and until P1.1a wave 3 this pass walked only the first two. So a
// field declared `as Nonexistent` reached no check at all, and `Parser.fieldStorageType`'s recovery
// value became the FINAL answer: the program compiled clean and the field silently typed itself
// `i64`. A wrong answer, not a crash.
//
// It also made `fieldStorageType`'s own comment false. That comment justified its recovery value by
// pointing here — "E3011, which `TypeResolution` reports against the merged registry, the
// authority... Returning `integer` keeps the op well-formed for the instant before the pipeline's
// error gate throws" — and nothing was reporting it, so there was no instant and no gate. The
// comment described the right design; the implementation never had it. This walk is what makes the
// sentence true, which is why the sentence stayed.
//
// ⚠ **IT VALIDATES AND DOES NOT REWRITE**, and there are two independent reasons — either alone
// would be sufficient:
//   • **Nothing reads a resolved field type.** The parser cannot wait for this pass (an op's type
//     must be concrete when it is EMITTED, and `resolveTypes` never rewrites an op's type), so it
//     resolves a field's storage type itself, through the sweep. A rewrite here would be a
//     mechanism with no consumer.
//   • **The layout object is the ARTIFACT's own.** `commitStructTypes` upserts it by REFERENCE and
//     an artifact is MEMOIZED, so writing through it would mutate a cached parse — the hazard
//     IrBlock's header states for the phi model, reached from a different direction.
// So `resolveType`'s RESULT is discarded and its DIAGNOSTIC is the point. `resolveNamedType` stays
// the single place that knows what a named type denotes; asking it here rather than re-deriving
// "is this name declared?" is what keeps the three declaration sites answering to one authority.
//
// ⚠ **IT CANNOT LIVE AT THE COMMIT POINT**, which is the tempting alternative (`commitStructTypes`,
// where the layouts arrive in declaration order and a Map walk would not be needed).
// `mergeArtifact` folds ONE artifact at a time, so a field naming a type declared in a file folded
// LATER would report a false E3011. The registry is complete only once every artifact is in, and
// that is exactly here.
//
// The walk is over the registry MAP, so its order is the map's rather than the source's — the one
// place this pass's diagnostics are not in declaration order. It is deterministic (the same program
// hashes the same names into the same slots), which is what the byte-identical-output gate needs;
// and a program with two undeclared field types has two independent bugs, not an ordering.
function checkStructFieldTypes(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
	for (_, layout) in project.structTypes 'eachStruct'
		for fieldType in layout.fieldTypes 'eachField'
			_ = resolveType(project, signatures: signatures, t: fieldType)
		end 'eachField'
	end 'eachStruct'
end 'checkStructFieldTypes'

// Re-sync a function's `funcSignatures` entry with its now-resolved param types.
// The parser recorded the signature with the SAME raw param types it put on
// `func.maxonParamTypes` (both from one param-parse), so the resolved
// `func.maxonParamTypes` above is exactly the concrete type list to write back; the
// param NAMES are untouched (resolution never renames a parameter). A function with
// no registered signature (none at M5 — the parser records one per function it
// builds) is left alone rather than fabricated.
//
// The pre-erasure `paramAggregateNames` column (OPEN #54 Slice B2) is carried STRAIGHT
// THROUGH — it is precisely the union/enum identity that resolving `func.maxonParamTypes`
// destroys (a `named` union collapses to `integer` here), so it must be read off the
// SIGNATURE (which the parser captured before this pass) and never re-derived from the
// now-erased param types.
function syncResolvedSignature(project Project, func IrFunction)
	if not project.funcSignatures.contains(func.name) 'noSig'
		return
	end 'noSig'
	let sig = try project.funcSignatures.get(func.name) otherwise panic("syncResolvedSignature: contains({String.from(func.name)}) true but get failed")
	project.funcSignatures.upsert(func.name, value: FuncSignature.create(sig.paramNames, paramTypes: sourceParamTypesOf(func, declaredCount: sig.paramNames.count()), paramAggregateNames: sig.paramAggregateNames))
end 'syncResolvedSignature'

// ⭐ **THE FUNCTION'S SOURCE PARAMETER TYPES — WHICH IS NOT ALWAYS ALL OF `maxonParamTypes`.** A LIFTED
// CLOSURE carries one more (`Parser.parseClosureExpression` promotes the reserved `__env` slot to a real
// trailing parameter so every function value shares one indirect signature), and its `funcSignatures` entry
// deliberately records the USER parameters only. Copying `maxonParamTypes` wholesale therefore left a
// closure's signature with 2 types against 1 name — the very lockstep `checkArgTypes` panics about, held by
// every other function and silently broken by this one.
//
// It went unnoticed while nothing asked a signature how many parameters its TYPES describe: `slotCallArgs`
// counts NAMES, and a lifted closure has no direct call to slot. It stops being invisible the moment a
// function value's declared SHAPE is read off the signature — a closure then claims an arity one too high
// and a `fn(int, int) returns int` where the source wrote `fn(int)`.
//
// The NAME column is the authority for how many source parameters there are, and the types are prefix-
// aligned with it by construction (the env is APPENDED), so the fix is a prefix. A fresh array is built only
// when the counts differ, i.e. only for a lifted closure.
function sourceParamTypesOf(func IrFunction, declaredCount ParamCount) returns MaxonTypeArray
	if func.maxonParamTypes.count() == declaredCount 'alreadyExact'
		return func.maxonParamTypes
	end 'alreadyExact'

	var sourceParams = MaxonTypeArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto declaredCount 'eachDeclared'
		sourceParams.push(try func.maxonParamTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("sourceParamTypesOf: '{String.from(func.name)}' declares {declaredCount} parameter names but its type column holds only {func.maxonParamTypes.count()} — the compiler may APPEND a hidden parameter (a lifted closure's `__env`), never omit a declared one"))
	end 'eachDeclared'
	return sourceParams
end 'sourceParamTypesOf'

function resolveReturnType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, ret MaxonReturnType) returns MaxonReturnType
	return match ret 'r'
		void gives MaxonReturnType.void
		value(inner) gives MaxonReturnType.value(resolveType(project, signatures: signatures, t: inner))
	end 'r'
end 'resolveReturnType'

function resolveType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, t MaxonType) returns MaxonType
	match t 'r'
		boolean then return t
		integer then return t
		float then return t
		exitCode then return t
		named(id) then return resolveNamedType(project, signatures: signatures, id: id)
		unresolved then panic("resolveTypes: `unresolved` reached type resolution — the parser emits no unresolved placeholders at M1")
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a `structRef`
		// directly for `Self` (and for the enclosing type's own name) inside a `type` body, because
		// that mapping is SYNTACTIC — it needs the enclosing declaration and no registry, so the
		// token sweep and the real parse can both make it and cannot drift. This arm is the OTHER
		// half of that: what `resolveNamedType` produces below for a `type` named from outside its
		// own body, arriving back here through a second `resolveTypes` over a re-resolved signature
		// would find it idempotent. Nothing to do either way.
		structRef then return t
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints `MaxonType.string`
		// directly for the `String` builtin (syntactically, like `Self` → `structRef`), so it never
		// arrives here as a `named` to resolve. This arm is what makes that concrete type idempotent
		// under a second `resolveTypes` — there is nothing to do.
		string then return t
		// Already resolved, and by the PARSER: `Parser.parseTypeReference`/`emitFunctionRef` mint
		// `MaxonType.function` directly (a function alias re-tagged, or a functionRef's own name), so it
		// never arrives as a `named` to resolve. Idempotent under a second `resolveTypes` — nothing to do.
		function then return t
		// Already concrete, and by the PARSER (P1.6-A): `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a
		// `typeParameter` for a `T` in scope and a `genericInstance` for a generic-instance alias
		// reference, both directly. There is no substitution at this trivial slice — a `T` stays `T` (an
		// opaque i64) and an instance stays an instance (a struct pointer) — so resolution leaves both
		// untouched, exactly as it leaves `structRef`/`string`/`function`.
		typeParameter then return t
		genericInstance then return t
		// Already concrete — `Parser.parseTypeReference` mints a `character` directly for the
		// `Character` builtin name, exactly as it mints `string` for `String`.
		character then return t
	end 'r'
end 'resolveType'

// ⭐ **THE TYPE NAMES THE COMPILER OWNS — a user declaration may not bind one (P1.7a slice 2b-ii).** Homed
// here because this is where the coupling lives: `resolveNamedType` below claims `ExitCode` and `HashValue`
// BEFORE it consults `structTypes`/`enumTypes`, and `createEnumRegistry` seeds `Ordering` INTO those
// registries — so under all three names the AUTHORITY's answer is fixed, while the PARSER's own `named`
// resolution (`resolveNamedStruct`, which reads the struct registry and knows nothing of these names) would
// happily follow a user declaration instead. That disagreement is not academic: **MEASURED — a user
// `type HashValue` made `Hashable.hash()`'s witness result a `structRef`, adopted as an owned struct box and
// dropped as one, faulting with 0xC0000005.** shv2 has no namespacing to keep a user's `Ordering` distinct
// from the builtin (the reference compilers do — theirs come from a stdlib MODULE), so the cure is to make
// the collision unrepresentable: `Parser.requireTypeNameNotCompilerOwned` refuses any declaration that would
// mint a NOMINAL identity under one of these names, which is what makes the arms below and the registry seed
// the SOLE writers of them. (A RANGED typealias over one is still legal — it erases to the same primitive
// the builtin does, so it cannot disagree; see that function.)
export function isCompilerOwnedTypeName(name ByteArray) returns bool
	return name.equals(ExitCodeBuiltinName) or name.equals(HashValueTypeName) or name.equals(OrderingTypeName)
end 'isCompilerOwnedTypeName'

// The name a `named` type denotes, ERASED to a concrete `MaxonType` — or `notDeclared` when it
// denotes nothing. THE one list of the sources a named type can come from; see this file's header
// for its three readers and for why it reads the signature index rather than `Project`.
//
// It THROWS rather than answering with a union case, and the reason is measured rather than
// stylistic: a Maxon union with an associated value is heap-boxed, this is asked once per named type
// reference in the whole program, and `RangedAliasRegistry.underlyingInAnyFile` already records what
// one such box per reference cost (+36 × 2^n allocations across the scale ladder — a term that grows
// with the PROGRAM, where every other cost of alias resolution grows only with the DECLARATIONS).
// `UnknownTypeName` is payload-free, so it is a scalar; the caller already holds the name.
export function denotedNamedType(signatures ProgramSignatures, name ByteArray, id TypeNameId) returns MaxonType throws UnknownTypeName
	if name.equals(ExitCodeBuiltinName) 'isExitCode'
		return MaxonType.exitCode
	end 'isExitCode'

	// ⭐ A COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED RANGED INT ALIAS — `HashValue`, the result type of `Hashable.hash()`. It is in
	// no alias registry (shv2 reads no stdlib module that declares it, so its
	// `typealias HashValue = int(0 to u32.max)` is in no registry), and without this arm it is E3011 on
	// every user `hash() returns HashValue`. It
	// resolves to width-free `integer` exactly as a ranged int alias does (`alias.underlying` drops the
	// range) — the u32 mask lives in the synthesized `int.hash` body, not the type. Handled inline like
	// `ExitCode` rather than seeded into `typeAliases`, which `resetMergeTargets` recreates empty per
	// warm-rebuild miss.
	//
	// The NAME SET is `isSynthesizedIntAliasName`'s, not this arm's, because `Parser.rangedAliasTag` reduces
	// the same names at parse time and the two answered differently until it was named once — see that
	// function's header for the `i.hash().hash()` this cost.
	if isSynthesizedIntAliasName(name) 'isSynthesizedIntAlias'
		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'isSynthesizedIntAlias'

	// ⭐ A DECLARED `type` KEEPS ITS NAME ID, and that one line is the difference between the two
	// registries this function consults. An alias RESOLVES AWAY — `Score` becomes `int`, and
	// nothing downstream can tell it was ever written — because an alias creates no nominal
	// distinction. A struct is nothing but a nominal distinction: `p.x` needs to know WHICH type
	// `p` is to find `x`'s slot (`Parser.emitFieldLoad` reads the id back out and asks
	// `structOf`), and two structs with identical fields are still two types. So the id survives,
	// carried by `structRef`.
	//
	// It is tested BEFORE the alias registry only for readability: a name cannot be both — a
	// `typealias` and a `type` of one name would be a duplicate declaration, and (⚠) that is NOT
	// yet diagnosed. It is a real gap, it belongs with the duplicate-`type` gap `commitStructTypes`
	// records, and neither is this rung's.
	// `containsStruct`, not `structOf`: the layout itself is not wanted here, and `structOf` boxes a
	// throwaway `DeclaredStruct.undeclared` for the COMMON no answer. Resolution answers WHICH TYPE
	// this name is, and the answer is the id the reference already carries. The fields are the
	// parser's question, asked of the signature index at the use site.
	if signatures.containsStruct(name) 'isStruct'
		return MaxonType.structRef(id)
	end 'isStruct'

	// ⭐ A DECLARED `enum`/`union` RESOLVES AWAY TO `integer`, the OPPOSITE of a struct — and for the
	// same reason an alias does. An enum value IS its i64 tag (int backing) or its float BIT PATTERN
	// (float backing), never a heap box, so the type carries no nominal identity downstream: after
	// resolution nothing needs to know a value was a `Color` rather than a bare int, because the
	// only thing that inspects an enum — `match` — reads the enum by NAME from the whole-program
	// signature index at PARSE time, long before this pass runs. So the id is dropped, exactly as a
	// ranged alias's is. (Float-backed enums resolve to `integer` too: the bits ARE an i64, matched
	// by an i64 compare — see EnumLayout.)
	//
	// The compiler-known `Ordering` resolves HERE rather than through an inline arm of its own, because
	// `createEnumRegistry` seeds it INTO this registry — one registration, every reader, no arm to add.
	if signatures.containsEnum(name) 'isEnum'
		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'isEnum'

	// A PLAIN ranged alias ("Score") — checked FIRST after the aggregates, and BEFORE the qualified-alias
	// path below, so the common case never pays the qualified split. A plain alias resolves to its underlying
	// primitive: two aliases over the same range are the same type, an alias creates no nominal distinction.
	//
	// ⚠ Bare-name (`underlyingInAnyFile`), and that is exactly right HERE rather than a gap: this arm
	// wants only `underlying`, and it DROPS the range — which is the erasure the whole type system
	// rests on. The RANGE is resolved per declaring file, where it is actually enforced
	// (`InsertRangeChecks`). A file to scope by is also genuinely absent on one of the two paths in:
	// `checkStructFieldTypes` resolves a `StructLayout`'s field types, and a layout records no
	// declaring file. This is the HOTTEST reader of the registry — once per named type reference in
	// the program — which is why the door it asks through hands back the primitive rather than a
	// `DeclaredAlias` that would be heap-boxed per call.
	if let underlying = try signatures.aliasUnderlyingInAnyFile(name) 'isPlainAlias'
		return underlying
	end 'isPlainAlias'

	// ⭐ A QUALIFIED ALIAS (P1.6-C) — a base-template inner alias ("Wrapper.Idx", the shared-body form a
	// generic method's `Idx` carries) OR a per-instance alias ("IntWrapper.Idx"). Both ERASE to their
	// underlying primitive here, EXACTLY as a plain ranged alias does — so `maxonTypeToStdType` never sees a
	// `named` residue in a signature or field type. The per-instance one's nominal name survives only in the
	// VALUE column, where the front-end identity checks read it; a signature carries no identity. Reached only
	// for a name that is NEITHER a plain alias nor an aggregate — a rare, dotted name — so the qualified-name
	// split never touches the plain-alias hot path.
	match signatures.qualifiedAliasOf(name) 'qualified'
		declared(alias) then return alias.underlying
		undeclared then break 'qualified'
	end 'qualified'

	// A name in NONE of the sources is a USER error — a typo (`Scor` for `Score`), a bare
	// sized type (`u8` is not a type in Maxon; a ranged alias over it is), or a
	// user-defined type this slice does not have. Each reader decides what to DO about it:
	// this pass reports E3011 unpositioned and recovers, the two cast sites raise the same
	// code anchored on the `as` they were written at.
	throw UnknownTypeName.notDeclared
end 'denotedNamedType'

// The one wording of "this name denotes no type", shared by the anchorless declaration sites below
// and by the `as`-cast sites in the parser (`Queries.reportParseError`'s `unknownCastTargetType`
// arm), so one fact has one text however it is reached.
export function unknownTypeMessage(typeName String) returns String
	return "Unknown type '{typeName}'"
end 'unknownTypeMessage'

// `denotedNamedType` for the DECLARATION sites this pass walks — a parameter, a return type, a
// struct field. Each reports and recovers rather than failing the pass, so a file with three
// undeclared types reports three diagnostics.
//
// UNPOSITIONED, exactly like E3001/E3002 — nothing records a source span for a type REFERENCE in a
// DECLARATION (only ops and parameter NAMES carry one), so there is no line to point at. Positioning
// it means threading that span from `parseTypeReference` through `FuncSignature` into the registry
// this pass reads, which is its own piece of work. ⚠ It is NOT a property of the diagnostic: a cast
// target reaches the same verdict POSITIONED, because the parser holds the `as` token when it asks.
function resolveNamedType(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, id TypeNameId) returns MaxonType
	let name = try project.typeNames.nameOf(id) otherwise panic("resolveTypes: named type id {id} is not in the project interner (merge did not fold this file's interner)")

	return try denotedNamedType(signatures, name: name, id: id) otherwise 'undeclared'
		reportDiagnostic(project, diagnostic: Diagnostic.create(ErrorCode.unknownType, message: unknownTypeMessage(String.from(name))))

		// A RECOVERY type, not a sentinel answer: `PassPipeline.run` gates on diagnostics
		// after EVERY pass and `resolveTypes` IS a pass, so the build throws the moment
		// this pass returns. Nothing downstream ever reads it — it exists only to keep
		// the module structurally well-formed for the instant in between, so that the
		// rest of this pass can go on to report the OTHER unknown types in the file
		// rather than stopping at the first.
		return MaxonType.integer
	end 'undeclared'
end 'resolveNamedType'
